hadiths[87]{hadithnumber,text}:
"155201","Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from his father that in a letter which the Messenger of Allah"
"155202","Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from his father that in a letter which the Messenger of Allah"
"155301","Malik related to me that he had heard that Umar ibn al-Khattab estimated the full blood-money for the people of urban areas. For those who had gold"
"155302","Malik related to me that he had heard that Umar ibn al-Khattab estimated the full blood-money for the people of urban areas. For those who had gold"
"155401","Malik narrated to me from Ibn Shihab from 'Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas from his father that he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to visit me during the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage because of an illness that had become severe. I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, the pain has reached such a degree as you see, and I am a man of wealth, and none inherits me except a single daughter. Shall I give two-thirds of my wealth in charity?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""No."" I said, ""Then half?"" He said, ""No."" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""One-third, and one-third is much. For you to leave your heirs wealthy is better than leaving them destitute, begging from people. And you will not spend any expenditure seeking thereby the Face of Allah except that you will be rewarded for it, even what you put into your wife's mouth."" I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, shall I be left behind after my companions?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""You will not be left behind to do any righteous deed except that you will increase thereby in degree and elevation. Perhaps you may be left behind so that some people may benefit by you and others be harmed by you. O Allah, cause the emigration of my companions to reach its goal and do not turn them back on their heels. But the wretched one is Sa'd ibn Khawla,"" and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pitied him because he died in Makkah."
"155402","Yahya related to me from Malik that Ibn Shihab said"
"155501","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said that Marwan ibn al-Hakam wrote to Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan that a madman was brought to him who had killed a man. Muawiya wrote to him"
"155502","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said that Marwan ibn al-Hakam wrote to Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan that a madman was brought to him who had killed a man. Muawiya wrote to him"
"155601","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Irak ibn Malik and Sulayman ibn Yasar that a man of the Banu Sad ibn Layth was running a horse and it trod on the finger of a man from the Juhayna tribe. It bled profusely"
"155602","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Irak ibn Malik and Sulayman ibn Yasar that a man of the Banu Sad ibn Layth was running a horse and it trod on the finger of a man from the Juhayna tribe. It bled profusely"
"155701","Mālik related to me, from Hishām ibn ‘Urwah, from his father, that a *mukhannath* (a man effeminate in appearance and manner) was with Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet, may Allāh bless him and grant him peace. He said to ‘Abdullāh ibn Abī Umayyah, while the Messenger of Allāh, may Allāh bless him and grant him peace, was listening: \O ‘Abdullāh"
"155702","Mālik related to me, from Abī al-Zinād, that he said: ‘Umar ibn ‘Abd al-‘Azīz flogged a slave eighty lashes for slander. Abī al-Zinād said: I asked ‘Abdullāh ibn ‘Āmir ibn Rabī‘ah about that, and he said: I lived during the time of ‘Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, ‘Uthmān ibn ‘Affān, and the Caliphs thereafter, and I did not see anyone flog a slave for slander more than forty lashes."
"155801","Malik related to me that the generally agreed on way of doing things amongst the community about an accident is that there is no blood-money until the victim is better. If a man's bone"
"155802","Malik related to me that the generally agreed on way of doing things amongst the community about an accident is that there is no blood-money until the victim is better. If a man's bone"
"155901","Yahya related to me from Malik that Ibn Shihab and also Urwa ibn az-Zubayr said the same as Said ibn al-Musayyab said about a woman. Her blood-money from a man is the same up to a third of the blood-money of a man. If what she is owed exceeds a third of the blood-money of the man"
"155902","Yahya related to me from Malik that Ibn Shihab and also Urwa ibn az-Zubayr said the same as Said ibn al-Musayyab said about a woman. Her blood-money from a man is the same up to a third of the blood-money of a man. If what she is owed exceeds a third of the blood-money of the man"
"156001","Malik narrated to me from Yahya ibn Sa'id that Abu al-Darda' wrote to Salman al-Farisi: ""Come to the Holy Land."" Salman wrote back to him: ""The land does not sanctify anyone; rather, a person is sanctified by his deeds. It has reached me that you have been made a physician to treat people. If you cure, then excellent for you; but if you are merely practicing medicine, then beware lest you kill a person and enter the Fire."" Abu al-Darda' would, whenever he judged between two people and they turned away from him, look at them and say, ""Return to me, repeat your case to me, for I am a mere practitioner, by Allah."" Yahya said: I heard Malik say: Whoever employs a slave without his master's permission in a matter of consequence, for which there is a standard wage, is liable for whatever harm befalls the slave. If the slave is harmed, the employer is liable; if the slave is unharmed and his master demands the wage for his work, that belongs to the master. This is the accepted ruling with us. I heard Malik say concerning a slave who is partly free and partly enslaved: his property is held in his own hand, but he may not dispose of it in any new way; he may eat and clothe himself from it in a reasonable manner. When he dies, his property belongs to the one in whom the slavery remains. I heard Malik say: The ruling with us is that a father may call his son to account for what he spent on him from the day the son possesses wealth — whether cash or goods — if the father wishes to do so."
"156002","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said that Abu'd-Darda wrote to
Salman al-Farsi, ""Come immediately to the holy land."" Salman wrote
back to him, ""Land does not make anyone holy. Man's deeds make him
holy. I have heard that you were put up as a doctor to treat and cure
people. If you are innocent, then may you have delight! If you are a
quack, then beware lest you kill a man and enter the Fire!"" When
Abu'd-Darda judged between two men, and they turned from him to go, he
would look at them and say, ""Come back to me, and tell me your story
again. A quack! By Allah!""


Yahya said that he heard Malik
say, ""If someone makes use of a slave, without permission of its
master, in anything important to him, whose like has a fee, he is
liable for what befalls the slave if anything befalls him. If the
slave is safe and his master asks for his wage for what he has done,
that is the master's right. This is what is done in our community.""


Yahya said that he heard Malik say about a slave who is part
free and part enslaved, ""His property is suspended in his hand and he
cannot begin anything with it. He eats from it and clothes himself in
an approved fashion. If he dies, his property belongs to the one to
whom he is in slavery.""


Yahya said that he heard Malik say,
""The way of doing things in our community is that a parent can take
his child to account for what he spends on him from the day the child
has property, cash or goods, if the parent wants that."""
"156101","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Abu Salama ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn Awf from Abu Hurayra that a woman from the Hudhayl tribe threw a stone at a woman from the same tribe"
"156102","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Abu Salama ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn Awf from Abu Hurayra that a woman from the Hudhayl tribe threw a stone at a woman from the same tribe"
"156201","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Said ibn al- Musayyab that the Messenger of Allah"
"156202","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Said ibn al- Musayyab that the Messenger of Allah"
"156301","Yahya related to me from Malik that Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman said"
"156302","Yahya related to me from Malik that Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman said"
"156401","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab that Said ibn al- Musayyab used to say"
"156402","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab that Said ibn al- Musayyab used to say"
"156501","Yahya related to me from Malik that he asked Ibn Shihab about the one-eyed man who gouged out the eye of a healthy person. Ibn Shihab said"
"156502","Yahya related to me from Malik that he asked Ibn Shihab about the one-eyed man who gouged out the eye of a healthy person. Ibn Shihab said"
"156601","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said from Sulayman ibn Yasar that Zayd ibn Thabit used to say"
"156602","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said from Sulayman ibn Yasar that Zayd ibn Thabit used to say"
"156701","Yahya related to me from Malik that Yahya ibn Said heard Sulayman ibn Yasar mention that a face wound in which the bone was bared was like a head wound in which the bone was bared"
"156702","Yahya related to me from Malik that Yahya ibn Said heard Sulayman ibn Yasar mention that a face wound in which the bone was bared was like a head wound in which the bone was bared"
"156801","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said"
"156802","Malik related to me from Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman that a man
in the time of Aban ibn Uthman's amirate freed all of his slaves and
did not have other property than them. Aban ibn Uthman took charge of
the slaves and they were divided into three groups. Then he drew lots
on the basis that which ever group drew the dead man's arrow would be
free. The arrow fell to one of the thirds, and that third was freed."
"156901","Malik narrated to me from Ibn Shihab that he heard him say: The established Sunnah is that when a slave is manumitted, his property follows him. Malik said: What further clarifies this is that when a mukatab (slave contracted for manumission) is contracted, his property follows him even if not stipulated, because the contract of kitaba is the contract of wala' (loyalty) when completed. The property of the slave and the mukatab is not like their children; their children are like their own persons, not like their property. The undisputed Sunnah is that when a slave is manumitted, his property follows him but his children do not; and when a mukatab is contracted, his property follows him but his children do not. Malik said: What also clarifies this is that if a slave or mukatab becomes bankrupt, their property and their umm walads (mothers of their children) are seized, but their children are not seized because they are not property. Malik said: What also clarifies this is that if a slave is sold and the buyer stipulates his property, his children do not enter into that property. Malik said: What also clarifies this is that if a slave commits a wounding offense, he and his property are seized, but his children are not seized."
"156902","Yahya related to me from Malik, from Yahya ibn Said, that it reached him that Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, Salim ibn Abdullah, and Urwah ibn Az-Zubayr used to say: If a runaway slave steals something for which the prescribed punishment is amputation, he is to be amputated."
"157001","Yahya related to me from Malik from Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman that Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr allowed retaliation for a head wound which splintered the bone"
"157002","Yahya related to me from Malik from Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman that Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr allowed retaliation for a head wound which splintered the bone"
"157101","Yahya related to me from Malik, that it reached him that a slave girl was brought to Umar ibn Al-Khattab, whose master had burned her with fire or injured her with it, and he freed her. Malik said: The established practice among us is that the emancipation of a man who has debts that encompass his wealth is not valid, and the emancipation of a boy is not valid until he reaches puberty or the age of puberty. And the emancipation of one under guardianship of his wealth is not valid, even if he reaches puberty, until he manages his own wealth."
"157102","Yahya related to me from Malik, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman, that Az-Zubayr ibn Al-Awwam met a man who had apprehended a thief and intended to take him to the ruler. Az-Zubayr interceded for him to let him go. The man said: No, until I take him to the ruler. Az-Zubayr said: When you take him to the ruler, may Allah curse the interceder and the one for whom intercession is made."
"157201","Yahya related to me from Malik from Zayd ibn Aslam from Muslim ibn Jundub from Aslam"
"157202","Yahya related to me from Malik from Zayd ibn Aslam from Muslim ibn Jundub from Aslam"
"157301","Yahya related to me from Malik that Yahya ibn Said heard Said ibn al-Musayyab say"
"157302","Yahya related to me from Malik that Yahya ibn Said heard Said ibn al-Musayyab say"
"157401","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said that Said ibn al-Musayyab used to say"
"157402","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said that Said ibn al-Musayyab used to say"
"157501","Yahya related to me from Malik from Da'ud ibn al-Husayn that Abu Ghatafan ibn Tarif al-Murri informed him that Marwan ibn al-Hakam sent him to Abdullah ibn Abbas to ask him what there was for the molar. Abdullah ibn Abbas said"
"157502","Yahya related to me from Malik from Da'ud ibn al-Husayn that Abu Ghatafan ibn Tarif al-Murri informed him that Marwan ibn al-Hakam sent him to Abdullah ibn Abbas to ask him what there was for the molar. Abdullah ibn Abbas said"
"157601","Yahya related to me from Malik from Hisham ibn Urwa that his father made all the teeth the same in the blood-money and did not prefer any kind over others. Malik said"
"157602","Yahya related to me from Malik from Hisham ibn Urwa that his father made all the teeth the same in the blood-money and did not prefer any kind over others. Malik said"
"157701","Yahya related to me from Malik that he had heard that Said ibn al-Musayyab and Sulayman ibn Yasar said"
"157702","Yahya related to me from Malik that he had heard that Said ibn al-Musayyab and Sulayman ibn Yasar said"
"157801","Malik related to me that he had heard that Marwan ibn al-Hakam gave a decision about a slave who was injured that the person who injured him had to pay what he had diminished of the value of the slave. Malik said"
"157802","Malik related to me that he had heard that Marwan ibn al-Hakam gave a decision about a slave who was injured that the person who injured him had to pay what he had diminished of the value of the slave. Malik said"
"157901","Yahya related to me from Malik that he heard that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz gave a decision that when a jew or christian was killed"
"157902","Yahya related to me from Malik that he heard that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz gave a decision that when a jew or christian was killed"
"158001","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said that Sulayman ibn Yasar said"
"158002","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said that Sulayman ibn Yasar said"
"158101","Malik related to me from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father that
A'isha, the wife of the Prophet, may Allah bless him and grant him
peace, said, ""Barira came to me and said, 'I have written myself as
mukatab for my people for nine uqiyas, one uqiya per year, so help
me.' A'isha said, 'If your people agree that I pay it all to them for
you, and that if I pay it, your wala' is mine, then I will do it.'
Barira went to her masters and told them that and they didn't agree.
She came back from her masters while the Messenger of Allah, may Allah
bless him and grant him peace, was sitting. She said to A'isha, 'I
offered that to them and they refused me unless they had the wala'.'
The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, heard
that and asked her about it A'isha told him and the Messenger of
Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said, 'Take her and
stipulate that the wala' is yours, for the wala' is for the one who
sets free.' So A'isha did that and then the Messenger of Allah, may
Allah bless him and grant him peace, stood up in front of the people,
and praised Allah and gave thanks to Him. Then he said, 'What is wrong
with the people who make conditions which are not in the Book of
Allah? Any condition which is not in the Book of Allah is invalid even
if it is a hundred conditions. The decree of Allah is truer and the
conditions of Allah are firmer, and the wala' only belongs to the one
who sets free.' """
"158102","Yahya related to me from Malik from Hisham ibn Urwa that his father said"
"158201","Yahya related to me from Malik that Ibn Shihab said"
"158202","Yahya related to me from Malik that Ibn Shihab said"
"1583","[1583] And he told me on the authority of Malik, on the authority of Al-Thiqah, on the authority of Bukair ibn Abdullah ibn Al-Ashjaj, on the authority of Abdul-Rahman ibn Al-Hubab Al-Ansari, on the authority of Abu Qatada Al-Ansari: The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade drinking dates and raisins together. And the beautiful and the fresh are all together. Muwatta Imam Malik Hadith: 1583 Arabic Hadith:"
"158401","Yahya related to me from Malik, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Abdullah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, forbade the sale of wala' and its gift."
"158402","Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Nafi', from Abdullah ibn Umar: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people during one of his military expeditions. Abdullah ibn Umar said: I approached him, but he departed before I reached him. I asked what he had said. It was said to me: He forbade that anything be steeped in gourds and in tarred vessels."
"158501","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab that Umar ibn al- Khattab demanded of the people at Mina"
"158502","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab that Umar ibn al- Khattab demanded of the people at Mina"
"158601","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Amr ibn Shuayb that a man of the Banu Mudlij called Qatada threw a sword at his son and it struck his thigh. The wound bled profusely and he died. Suraqa ibn Jusham came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and mentioned that to him Umar said to him"
"158602","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Amr ibn Shuayb that a man of the Banu Mudlij called Qatada threw a sword at his son and it struck his thigh. The wound bled profusely and he died. Suraqa ibn Jusham came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and mentioned that to him Umar said to him"
"158701","Malik narrated to me that it reached him that Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab was asked about a slave who had children by a free woman: to whom does their wala' belong? Sa'id said: If their father dies while still a slave, not manumitted, their wala' belongs to the mawali (patrons) of their mother. Malik said: The same applies to the child of li'an (mutual imprecation) from the mawali: he is attributed to the mawali of his mother, so they are his mawali. If he dies, they inherit him; if he commits a crime, they pay his blood money. If his father acknowledges him, he is attached to him and his wala' goes to his father's mawali; his inheritance belongs to them and his blood money is upon them, and his father is flogged the hadd. Malik said: Likewise, a woman from the Arabs who engages in li'an: if her husband who imprecated her acknowledges her child, he assumes the same status, except that the remainder of his inheritance after his mother's and maternal siblings' shares goes to the Muslims in general, unless he is attached to his father. The child of li'an inherits the wala' of his mother's mawali before his father acknowledges him because he had no lineage or 'asaba (paternal relatives); when his lineage is established, he goes to his 'asaba. Malik said: The agreed ruling with us regarding the child of a slave by a free woman, when the slave's father is free, is that the grandfather (the slave's father) draws the wala' of his son's free children by a free woman; he inherits them as long as their father is a slave. If their father is manumitted, the wala' reverts to his mawali. If he dies while a slave, the inheritance and wala' belong to the grandfather. If the slave has two free sons and one dies while the father is a slave, the grandfather draws the wala' and inheritance. Malik said concerning a slave woman who is manumitted while pregnant, her husband being a slave, then the husband is manumitted before or after she gives birth: the wala' of what was in her womb belongs to the one who manumitted the mother, because that child was affected by slavery before the mother's manumission. He is not like the child conceived after manumission, for if the father is manumitted, he draws that child's wala'. Malik said concerning a slave who asks his master's permission to manumit a slave of his own, and the master permits: the wala' of the manumitted slave belongs to..."
"158702","Malik said that he had heard that Said ibn al-Musayyab and Sulayman ibn Yasar were asked"
"158801","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Urwa ibn az- Zubayr that a man of the Ansar called Uhayha ibn al-Julah had a young paternal uncle who was younger than him and who was living with his maternal uncles. Uhayha took him and killed him. His maternal uncles said"
"158802","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Urwa ibn az- Zubayr that a man of the Ansar called Uhayha ibn al-Julah had a young paternal uncle who was younger than him and who was living with his maternal uncles. Uhayha took him and killed him. His maternal uncles said"
"158901","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Said ibn al- Musayyab and Abu Salama ibn Abd ar-Rahman from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah"
"158902","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Said ibn al- Musayyab and Abu Salama ibn Abd ar-Rahman from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah"
"159001","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that Umar ibn al-Khattab killed five or seven people for one man whom they had killed secretly by trickery. Umar said"
"159002","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that Umar ibn al-Khattab killed five or seven people for one man whom they had killed secretly by trickery. Umar said"
"159101","Yahya related to me from Malik from Muhammad ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn Sad ibn Zurara that he had heard that Hafsa"
"159102","Yahya related to me from Malik from Muhammad ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn Sad ibn Zurara that he had heard that Hafsa"
"159201","Yahya related to me from Malik from Umar ibn Husayn"
"159202","Malik related to me from Nafi that Abdullah ibn Umar said, ""A
mukatab is a slave as long as any of his kitaba remains to be paid."""
"159301","Yahya related to me from Malik that he had heard that Marwan ibn al-Hakam wrote to Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan to mention to him that a drunkard was brought to him who had killed a man. Muawiya wrote to him to kill him in retaliation for the dead man. Yahya said that Malik said"
"159302","Yahya related to me from Malik that he had heard that Marwan ibn al-Hakam wrote to Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan to mention to him that a drunkard was brought to him who had killed a man. Muawiya wrote to him to kill him in retaliation for the dead man. Yahya said that Malik said"
"159401","Malik narrated to me from Humayd ibn Qays al-Makki that a mukatab belonging to Ibn al-Mutawakkil died in Makkah, leaving a remaining balance of his kitaba, debts to people, and a daughter. The governor of Makkah found the judgment problematic, so he wrote to 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan asking about it. 'Abd al-Malik wrote back: ""Begin with the debts of the people, then pay off the remainder of his kitaba, then divide whatever remains of his wealth between his daughter and his mawla."" Malik said: The ruling with us is that it is not obligatory for a master to contract a mukataba with his slave if he asks for it. I have not heard that any of the Imams compelled a man to contract a mukataba with his slave. I have heard some people of knowledge, when asked about this, recite the verse: {And if you know any good in them, make a contract with them} (24:33), then recite these two verses: {And when you are secure, then hunt} (5:2) and {Then when the prayer is finished, disperse through the land and seek the bounty of Allah} (62:10). Malik said: That is merely a permission Allah has granted to people, not an obligation upon them. Malik said: I heard some people of knowledge say regarding Allah's words: {And give them from the wealth of Allah which He has given you} (24:33): This means a man contracts a mukataba with his slave, then remits a specified portion of the final payment. Malik said: This is what I heard from the people of knowledge and found the practice of people upon in our land. Malik said: It has reached me that 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar contracted a mukataba with a slave of his for 35,000 dirhams, then remitted 5,000 dirhams from the final payment. Malik said: The ruling with us is that when a master contracts a mukataba with his slave, the slave's property follows him, but his children do not, unless he stipulates them in the kitaba. Yahya said: I heard Malik say concerning a mukatab whose master contracts him while he has a slave girl pregnant by him, unknown to both on the day of the contract: that child does not follow him (into the kitaba) because it was not included in the contract; it belongs to the master. As for the slave girl, she belongs to the mukatab because she is part of his property. Malik said concerning a man who inherits a mukatab from his wife, he and her son: If the mukatab dies before fulfilling his kitaba, they divide his inheritance according to the Book of Allah. If he fulfills his kitaba then dies, his inheritance belongs to the woman's son, and the husband has no share. Malik said concerning a mukatab who contracts his own slave: It is examined. If he only intended to favor his slave, known by his leniency, it is not permitted. If he contracted him seeking profit and bounty, and to assist in his own kitaba, that is permitted. Malik said concerning a man who has intercourse with his mukataba: If she becomes pregnant, she has the choice: if she wishes, she becomes an umm walad; if she wishes, she remains on her kitaba. If she does not become pregnant, she remains on her kitaba. Malik said: The agreed ruling with us regarding a slave owned by two men is that neither may contract a mukataba for his share, whether the other permits it or not, unless they both contract it together. Because that would constitute a contract of manumission for a share, and when the slave fulfills what he was contracted for, half of him becomes free, and the one who contracted part of him would not be obliged to complete his manumission. That contradicts what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever manumits a share in a slave, a fair price is estimated for him."" Malik said: If he did this ignorantly until the mukatab fulfills it, or before he fulfills it, the one who contracted him returns what he took from the mukatab, they divide it according to their shares, the kitaba is void, and the slave remains as before. Malik said concerning a mukatab owned by two men: one grants him respite for his due, the other refuses and exacts part of his due. Then the mukatab dies leaving wealth insufficient to fulfill the kitaba. Malik said: They share proportionally to what remains due to them; each takes according to his share. If the mukatab leaves surplus beyond the kitaba, each takes what remains of the kitaba, and the surplus is divided equally between them. If the mukatab becomes unable to pay, and the one who did not grant respite exacted more than his partner, the slave is divided equally between them, and the one who exacted more does not return the excess to his partner, because he exacted his due with his partner's permission. If one of them remits his due, then the other exacts part of his due, then the mukatab becomes unable, the slave is between them, and the one who exacted does not return anything to his partner, because he exacted what was due to him. This is like a debt owed by one man to two men under a single contract: one grants respite, the other presses, exacts part, then the debtor goes bankrupt. The one who exacted is not obliged to return anything he took."
"159402","Yahya related to me from Malik that he saw whomever he was satisfied with among the people of knowledge say about a man who willed that his murderer be pardoned when he murdered him intentionally"
"159501","Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Qatan ibn Wahb ibn Umayr ibn al-Ajda'."
"159502","Yahya narrated to me from Malik from Qatan ibn Wahb ibn 'Umayr ibn al-Ajda' that Yuhannis, the mawla of al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam, informed him: He was sitting with 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar during the fitna (civil strife). A mawla of his came to greet him and said: ""I intend to leave, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, for times have become hard upon us."" 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar said to her: ""Stay put, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'No one endures its hardships and severity except that I will be an intercessor or a witness for him on the Day of Resurrection.'"" Muwatta Imam Malik, Hadith 1595."
